nce battles another deadly  fourth wave i believe that the  leadership in this province has been  wanting it's been all over the map and  inconsistent it's clear that uh  albertans  and party members cannot trust him on  tuesday afternoon kenny decided to shake  things up swapping out his health  minister the timing no coincidence but  this political analyst says the federal  election results are not why having  stuck with the health minister for this  long and only replacing him on the eve  of a caucus meeting that is rumored to  have a non-confidence motion at it  really does suggest that the premier is  throwing minister chandra under the bus  in an attempt to save his own career  there is another factor that could have  weakened tory support in alberta while  they didn't win a seat the people's  party of canada got a little over 7  percent of the popular vote here  further proof of a shift in a province  often reluctant to change  jamie dahl global news edmonton  ahead how monday's vote paints another  clear picture of canada's stark  divisions  the outcome of the federal election  exposed a sharp divide in this country  prime minister trudeau's political  gamble didn't get him the majority he  wanted and was an unpopular move among  many canadians the outcome appears to  have changed little but it did stoke  anger here's eric sorenson  donna this is what the house of commons  looked like at disillusion and after  yesterday's election so far it looked  like this  you could be forgiven for not seeing  much difference mr trudeau was hoping  for a quick power grab it wasn't the  most gracious concession but aaron  o'toole's reaction fueled by that  election outcome underscores the  divisions in the country canadians sent  him back with another minority at the  cost of 600 million dollars and deeper  divisions in our great country some have  talked about division  but that's not what i see the prime  minister was already trying to move on  but the campaign wounds an